M|                                      "REAR WINDOW"
O|
M|                                            by
O|
M|                                    John Michael Hayes
O|
M|                                  Based on a short story
O|
M|                                            by
O|
M|                                     Cornell Woolrich
O|
M|                                       Final Draft
O|
M|                                     December 1, 1953
O|
O|                
O|
T|               FADE IN:
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFFERIES' APARTMENT - DAY - LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               Although we do not see the foreground window frame, we see 
N|               the whole background of a Greenwich Village street.
O|
N,G|               We can see the rear of a number of assorted houses and small 
N|               apartment buildings whose fronts face on the next cross-town 
N|               street, sharply etched by the morning sun.
O|
N|               Some are two stories high; others three; some have peaked 
N|               roofs, others are flat. There is a mixture of brick and wood 
N|               and wrought iron in the construction.
O|
N|               The apartment buildings have fire escapes, the others do 
N|               not.
O|
N|               The neighborhood is not a prosperous one, but neither is it 
N|               poor. It is a practical, conventional dwelling place for 
N|               people living on marginal incomes, luck -- or hope and careful 
N|               planning.
O|
N|               The summer air is motionless and heavy with humid heat.
O|
N|               It has opened windows wide, pushed back curtains, lifted 
N|               blinds and generally brought the neighborhood life into a 
N|               sweltering intimacy. Yet, people born and bred to life within 
N|               earshot and eye glance of a score of neighbors have learned 
N|               to preserve their own private worlds by uniformly ignoring 
N|               each other, except on direct invitation.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PULLS BACK until a large sleeping profile of a 
N,G|               man fills the screen. It is so large that we do not see any 
N|               features, but merely the temple and side of the cheek down 
N|               which a stream of sweat is running.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PANS OFF this to the right hand side of the window, 
N,G|               and MOVES TO a thermometer which is hanging on the wall just 
N|               outside the window. It registers 84.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA MOVES ON into the open, and brings nearer to us a 
N,G|               room with a large studio window. We are able to see inside 
N|               this room. A short, balding man is standing near the window, 
N|               shaving, using a small bowl of water and a portable mirror 
N|               which he has set up on a shelf.
O|
N|               To the right of him is a battered upright piano. On top of 
N|               the piano is a radio. The music selection coming from the 
N|               radio stops, and the announcer is heard.
O|
C|                                     ANNOUNCER
U|                         The time -- 7:15 A.M., WOR, New York. 
U|                         The temperature, outside, 84 -- 
U|                         Friends -- is your life worth one 
U|                         dollar?
O|
N|               The man shaving quickly puts down his razor, hurries to the 
N|               radio, and changes the station, moving past a number of 
N|               commercial voices until he again finds some music.
O|
N|               Contented, he returns to his shaving.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA MOVES ON AND OVER to a far building. It passes 
N,G|               over the face of this building until it comes to fire escapes. 
N,G|               It goes up and near enough to one which has become the outdoor 
N,G|               bedroom of a couple. We are near enough to see an alarm clock 
N|               hanging from the rail which is now ringing vigorously. A man 
N|               rises lazily to a sitting position. He gropes to switch the 
N|               alarm off.
O|
N,G|               We see that his pajamas are stained with sweat. In his sitting 
N|               position he leans forward and shakes somebody beside him. To 
N|               our surprise, the head of this other person -- a woman -- 
N|               rises where his feet are. They have been sleeping in opposite 
N|               directions. They sit limply looking at each other with 
N|               bedraggled and weary expressions which show they enjoyed 
N|               very little sleep in the heat of the night.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA NOW MOVES DOWN toward the left onto another low 
N,G|               building. It MOVES IN A LITTLE to a living room window. Just 
N|               inside the windowsill, a small fan is oscillating. The fan 
N|               sits on the right side of the table, and to the left of it 
N|               is an automatic toaster.
O|
N|               Behind the toaster stands a full-bodied young woman, 
N|               apparently wearing only a pair of black panties. Her stomach, 
N|               navel, and the lower part of her chest are naked. Just below 
N|               her breasts, the curtain, partly drawn, has thrown a deep 
N|               shadow which extends upward, hiding her breasts, shoulders 
N|               and head. Two pieces of toast pop up in the toaster. She 
N|               takes them out, butters them. Then she turns around and bends 
N|               over another table on which stands an automatic coffee-maker.
O|
N|               She picks up the coffee-maker, and swings back to the table 
N|               to sit down. She does this so deftly that her breasts are 
N|               never exposed, but hidden by the fan as she sits down. The 
N|               fan moves back and forth as she pours coffee, far enough to 
N|               reveal that she wears no bra, but not far enough to fulfill 
N|               the exciting promise of her lack of clothes.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA MOVES ON to a distant street corner seen between 
N|               two buildings. The traffic is very light at this hour, but a 
N|               Sanitation Department truck moves through the intersection 
N|               spraying water out behind it to cool the pavement and keep 
N|               the dust down. Three little kids in bathing suits run behind 
N|               the truck, playing in the water.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA MOVES OFF and around to some buildings at the 
N,G|               side. As it skims this building, we see a hand emerge from 
N|               one of the windows, and remove the cover from a birdcage 
N|               which is hanging from a hook on the wall outside. In the 
N|               cage are two lovebirds -- arguing.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA NOW PULLS BACK SWIFTLY and retreats through the 
N,G|               open window back into Jefferies' apartment. We now see more 
N,G|               of the sleeping man. THE CAMERA GOES IN far enough to show a 
N|               head and shoulders of him.
O|
N,I|               He is L. B. JEFFERIES. A tall, lean, energetic thirty five, 
N,I|               his face long and serious-looking at rest, is in other 
N|               circumstances capable of humor, passion, naive wonder and 
N|               the kind of intensity that bespeaks inner convictions of 
N|               moral strength and basic honesty.
O|
N|               He is sitting in an Everest and Jennings wheelchair.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PANS along his right leg. It is encased in a 
N|               plaster of Paris spica from his waistline to the base of his 
N|               toes. Along the white cast someone has written "Here lie the 
N|               broken bones of L. B. Jefferies."
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PANS to a nearby table on which rests a shattered 
N|               and twisted Speed Graphic Camera, the kind used by fast-action 
N|               news photographers.
O|
N,G|               On the same table, the CAMERA PANS to an eight by ten glossy 
N|               photo print. It shows a dirt track auto racing speedway, 
N|               taken from a point dangerously near the center of the track. 
N|               A racing car is skidding toward the camera, out of control, 
N|               spewing a cloud of dust behind it. A rear wheel has come off 
N|               the car, and the wheel is bounding at top speed directly 
N|               into the camera lens.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA MOVES UP to a framed photograph on the wall.
O|
N|               It is a fourteen by ten print, an essay in violence, having 
N|               caught on film the exploding semi-second when a heavy 
N|               artillery shell arches into a front-line Korean battle 
N|               outpost. Men and equipment erupt into the air suspended in a 
N|               solution of blasted rock, dust and screeching shrapnel. That 
N|               the photographer was not a casualty is evident, but surprising 
N|               when the short distance between the camera and the explosion 
N|               is estimated. A signature in the lower right hand corner of 
N|               the picture reads -- "L. B. Jefferies."
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PANS to a second photograph of a picket line at 
N|               an aircraft plant strike. Strikers, non-strikers and police 
N|               are embroiled in a bitter and confused riot.
O|
N|               Clubs, fists and truncheons swing, blood flows, faces twist 
N|               with emotion and fallen victims struggle to regain their 
N|               feet. The picture represents no distant, cautions photographic 
N|               observation, but rather an intimate report, so immediate and 
N|               real that the viewer has the nervous feeling the fight 
N|               surrounds him and he had best defend himself. The same 
N|               signature, "L. B. Jefferies," is in the corner.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PANS TO another framed picture, this one a 
N|               beautiful and awesome shot of an atomic explosion at 
N|               Frenchman's Flat, Nevada. It is the cul-de-sac of violence. 
N|               The picture taken at a distant observation point, shows some 
N|               spectators in the foreground watching the explosion through 
N|               binoculars.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA MOVES ON to a shelf containing a number of cameras, 
N,G|               photographic film, etc. It then PAN ACROSS a large viewer on 
N|               which is resting a negative of a woman's head.
O|
N,G|               From this, THE CAMERA MOVES ON to a magazine cover, and 
N,G|               although we do not see the name of the magazine, we can see 
N|               the head on the cover is the positive of the negative we 
N|               have just passed.
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA FINALLY COMES TO REST ON a pile of magazines -- 
N|               perhaps a hundred or so. They are all of the same publication.
O|
T|                                                           LAP DISSOLVE TO:
O|
S,G|               INT. GUNNISON'S OFFICE - DAY - CLOSE UP
O|
N,G|               The screen is filled with the top of a desk. In addition to 
N|               the usual telephones, blotting pad, etc., the most prominent 
N|               feature is the number of glossy photo prints, and even larger-
N|               sized mat prints. Some of them have slips pasted over with 
N|               descriptions. The center of the desk is occupied by a large 
N|               layout of photographs on one magazine page. Behind this we 
N|               hear the murmur of two voices of men who can be vaguely seen 
N|               beyond the desk.
O|
N,G,I|               THE CAMERA PANS UP and we are now face to face with IVAR 
N,I|               GUNNISON and JACK BRYCE. Gunnison is sitting on a window-
N|               ledge, and beyond him we realize we are high above the New 
N|               York streets. Bryce leans against a wall at right angles to 
N|               him.
O|
N|               Gunnison is holding a cablegram in his hand. Bryce has a 
N|               cigarette in his mouth. He scratches a match, and is about 
N|               to light it, when he notices that Gunnison, still reading 
N|               the cable, has reached into an inside shirt pocket, and 
N|               produced a cigarette. Quickly, Bryce moves over to light 
N|               Gunnison's cigarette. Then he settles back to light his own. 
N|               Gunnison doesn't even bother to thank him.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (Looks up)
U|                         Indo-China -- Jeff predicted it would 
U|                         go sky-high.
O|
C|                                     BRYCE
U|                         From the looks of Davidson's cable, 
U|                         it might even go higher than that. 
U|                         And we haven't even got a camera 
U|                         over there.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (Stands)
U|                         This could go off in a month -- or 
U|                         an hour.
O|
C|                                     BRYCE
U|                         I'll pull somebody out of Japan.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (Heads for his phone)
U|                         Bryce, the only man for this job is 
U|                         sitting right here in town.
P|                              (Picks up phone)
U|                         Get me L. B. Jefferies.
O|
C|                                     BRYCE
P|                              (Puzzled)
U|                         Jefferies?
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (To Bryce; still 
P|                              holding phone)
U|                         Name me a better photographer.
O|
C|                                     BRYCE
P|                              (He can't)
U|                         But his leg!
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         Don't worry -- it comes off today.
O|
N|               Bryce gives Gunnison a startled look.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         I mean the cast.
P|                              (To phone)
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N,G|               Shooting through the open window, onto Jeff. He is shaving 
N|               himself with an electric razor as the phone rings. He shuts 
N|               off the shaver, picks up the phone.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Jefferies.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (On filter)
U|                         Congratulations, Jeff.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         For what?
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         For getting rid of that cast.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Who said I was getting rid of it?
O|
N|               At this moment, his attention is drawn to something across 
N|               the way. He looks up, expectantly. There is almost a touch 
N|               of eagerness in his expression.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               While Jeff is continuing his phone conversation, we see the 
N,G|               object of his look. Two pretty girls have appeared on the 
N|               distant roof. They are smiling and talking, although we cannot 
N|               hear their dialogue. Each wears a terrycloth robe. With their 
N,G|               backs to the CAMERA, they take off the robes, slipping them 
N|               down over their shoulders slowly. Then, seductively, they 
N|               turn -- revealing the full beauty of their tanned and bathing-
N|               suited bodies. It is almost as if they want to be noticed, 
N|               the center of neighborhood attention. They at least have all 
N|               of Jeff's attention. Then they spread the robes in front of 
N|               them, and lie down on the roof, and out of sight. Jeff seems 
N|               a little disappointed.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N|               During the whole of this previous action, the conversation 
N|               between Jeff and Gunnison has gone on as follows:
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (With logical proof)
U|                         This is Wednesday.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Gunnison -- how did you get to be 
U|                         such a big editor -- with such a 
U|                         small memory?
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         Wrong day?
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Wrong week. Next Wednesday I emerge 
U|                         from this plaster cocoon.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         That's too bad, Jeff. Well, I guess 
U|                         I can't be lucky every day. Forget I 
U|                         called.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Yeah. I sure feel sorry for you, 
U|                         Gunnison. Must be rough on you 
U|                         thinking of me wearing this cast 
U|                         another whole week.
O|
S,G|               INT. GUNNISON'S OFFICE - DAY - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Gunnison is now seated at his desk, with the phone receiver 
N|               to his ear. His assistant, Bryce, can be seen vaguely in the 
N|               background.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         That one week is going to cost me my 
U|                         best photographer -- and you a big 
U|                         assignment.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - CLOSE-UP
O|
N|               Jeff asks, eagerly and alertly.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Where?
O|
N|               We hear Gunnison's reply.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         There's no point in even talking 
U|                         about it.
O|
N|               Jeff's eyes become set upon something else in the neighborhood 
N|               he sees.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - LONG SHOT
O|
N|               Jeff's attention is now drawn to another feature of his 
N,G|               backyard entertainment. THE CAMERA IS NOW FOCUSED on the 
N|               window of the small building where we earlier saw the girl 
N|               behind the oscillating fan. Loud ballet music is pouring 
N|               from her open window. The girl, now dressed in dark and 
N|               revealing leotard, and ballet slippers, has just turned away 
N|               from a portable record player. She begins the first graceful 
N|               movement of a modern ballet interpretation.
O|
N|               She gracefully moves across the room to the rhythm of the 
N|               music and dance, toward the ice box. With her feet still 
N|               moving, she throws open the door, and then rhythmically moving 
N|               back to the center of the room, gnaws the chicken bone, 
N|               occasionally waving it in the air as part of the choreography. 
N|               She now twirls over toward a table at the other side of the 
N|               room on which is an open package of bread slices, some butter 
N|               nearby.
O|
N|               With swaying body, she puts down the chicken leg, and 
N|               gracefully and rhythmically butters a slice of bread.
O|
N|               She picks up both bread and chicken leg and continues her 
N|               interpretive dance, alternately munching the bread and butter 
N|               and chicken leg.
O|
S,G|               INT. APARTMENT - DAY - CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Jeff's eyes drop from the ballet dancer's room to the one 
N|               underneath.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PANS from the window of the dancing girl, to the 
N|               window below. Someone is reading the New York Herald Tribune. 
N,G|               The paper lowers, and we see an elderly lady, in her late 
N|               sixties. She is a faded, refined type. She looks up in the 
N|               direction of the music and in a calm routine fashion adjusts 
N|               the volume of her hearing aid. She resumes her reading.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Jeff is amused by what he sees, but continues his conversation 
N|               with Gunnison, which has gone on through all the scenes with 
N|               the ballet dancer.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (Insistent)
U|                         Where?
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (Filter)
U|                         Indo-China. Got a code tip from the 
U|                         bureau chief this morning. The place 
U|                         is about to go up in smoke.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (Pleased; excited)
U|                         Didn't I tell you! Didn't I tell you 
U|                         it was the next place to watch?
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         You did.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (On filter)
U|                         Okay. When do I leave? Half-hour? An 
U|                         hour?
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         With that cast on -- you don't.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (On filter)
U|                         Stop sounding stuffy. I'll take 
U|                         pictures from a jeep. From a water 
U|                         buffalo if necessary.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         You're too valuable to the magazine 
U|                         for us to play around with. I'll 
U|                         send Morgan or Lambert.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Swell. I get myself half-killed for 
U|                         you -- and you reward me by stealing 
U|                         my assignments.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         I didn't ask you to stand in the 
U|                         middle of that automobile race track.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (A little angry)
U|                         You asked for something dramatically 
U|                         different! You got it!
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
P|                              (Quietly)
U|                         So did you. Goodbye, Jeff.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (Won't let him hang 
P|                              up)
U|                         You've got to get me out of here! 
U|                         Six weeks -- sitting in a two-room 
U|                         apartment with nothing to do but 
U|                         look out the window at the neighbors!
O|
N|               At this moment we hear the sounds of a piano playing.
O|
N|               It is a simple, but broken, melody as if someone was just 
N|               learning to play the piano, or carefully composing a song. 
N|               It clashes abruptly with the music from the ballet dancer's 
N|               apartment. It irritates Jeff as he looks in the direction of 
N|               the new music.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         It's worse than the Chinese water 
U|                         torture.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - SEMI-LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               We now see the source of the piano music. It comes from the 
N|               apartment with the studio window which we saw earlier where 
N|               the man was shaving and listening to the radio. The short, 
N|               balding man sits at the piano playing a few notes, then 
N|               transferring them by pencil to notepaper on the piano rack. 
N|               He continues this process, fighting the interference of the 
N|               ballet music. The opening bars of his melody are beautiful 
N|               and ear-catching.
O|
N|               It is slow, hard work, and the ballet music finally becomes 
N|               such an interference that he gives up and walks to the window 
N|               to look down toward the dancer's apartment.
O|
N|               He stands by a table at the window which is littered with 
N|               records, the morning coffee cup, unwashed, the remains of 
N|               breakfast, old newspapers, song sheets, etc.
O|
N|               He takes a cigarette out of his mouth, looks for an ash tray, 
N|               and ends up putting it out in the coffee cup. He then returns 
N|               to the piano and begins picking out the melody the dancer is 
N|               playing on her record player.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Jeff frowns at the double sound, and raises his voice a 
N|               little. He continues the conversation which has been heard 
N|               all through the previous scene.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         Read some good books.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         I've been taking pictures so long I 
U|                         don't know how to read anymore. 
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         I'll send you some comic books.  
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (Low, tense)
U|                         Listen -- if you don't pull me out 
U|                         of this swamp of boredom -- I'll do 
U|                         something drastic.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         Like what?
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (On filter)
U|                         I'll -- I'll get married. Then I'll 
U|                         never be able to go anywhere.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         It's about time you got married -- 
U|                         before you turn into a lonesome and 
U|                         bitter old man.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Can you see me -- rushing home to a 
U|                         hot apartment every night to listen 
U|                         to the automatic laundry, the electric 
U|                         dishwasher, the garbage disposal and 
U|                         a nagging wife.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         Jeff -- wives don't nag anymore -- 
U|                         they discuss.
O|
N|               Jefferies glances out across to the other apartments as he 
N|               sees:
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - SEMI-LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               We see a three-storied, flat-roofed apartment house.
O|
N|               The brick is weather-worn and faded. Each apartment has three 
N|               windows facing the back, one showing a hallway, one a living 
N|               room, and the window on the right opening into a bedroom.
O|
N|               On the second floor, a man has entered the living room from 
N|               a hallway door. He carries a large aluminum sample case common 
N|               to salesmen. He sets down the case heavily, removes his hat, 
N|               and slowly wipes his brow with the back of his right hand. 
N|               He takes off his coat and tie. His shirt is stained with 
N|               sweat underneath. He rolls up his sleeves, and his well-
N|               muscled arms heavy with hair confirm his dark, husky build.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N|               With his eyes still focused on the distant apartments, Jeff 
N|               continues talking with Gunnison.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Yeah? Maybe in the high rent districts 
U|                         they discuss -- but in my 
U|                         neighborhood, they still nag.
O|
C|                                     GUNNISON
U|                         Well -- you know best. Call you later, 
U|                         Jeff.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Next time, have some good news.
O|
N|               He hangs up and resumes his attention on the apartment of 
N|               the salesman.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - SEMI-LONG SHOT
O|
N|               The salesman looks toward the bedroom door, hesitates, then 
N|               reluctantly walks toward it. For a moment he is hidden by 
N|               the wall.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Jeff shifts his look more to the right.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - SEMI-LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               The man enters the bedroom. We can see a woman lying on the 
N|               far bed. Near her, a small table is covered with medicine 
N|               bottles, spoons, boxes of pills, a water pitcher and the 
N|               other impedimenta of the chronically ill. The woman sits up 
N|               as the man enters. She takes a wet cloth off her forehead. 
N|               Before the man even reaches her, she begins talking, somewhat 
N|               vigorously. Pointing to a wristwatch, she seems to be saying 
N|               something such as "You should have been home two hours ago! 
N|               I could be lying here dying for all you'd know -- or care!" 
N|               The man stops short of the bed, makes gestures of trying to 
N|               placate her, but she goes on scolding. His attitude changes 
N|               to weary patience, then irritation, then anger.
O|
N|               He shouts back at her, turns and goes out of the room.
O|
N|               Back in the living room, he picks up his hat, throws it 
N|               against the wall in anger, and leaves the apartment, slamming 
N|               the door behind him.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - MEDIUM SHOT
O|
N|               Jeff's attention is suddenly diverted to himself. His leg, 
N|               under the cast, begins itching. He squirms, tries to move 
N|               the leg a little. It gives no relief. He scratches the outside 
N|               of the cast, but the itch gets worse. He reaches for a long, 
N|               Chinese back scratcher lying on the windowsill. Carefully, 
N|               and with considerable ingenuity, he works it under the cast. 
N|               He scratches, and a look of sublime relief comes over his 
N|               face. Satisfied, he takes the scratcher out. As he replaces 
N|               it on the windowsill, his attention is drawn back to the 
N|               scene outside the window.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - SEMI-LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               We see the man who left his apartment in anger come out of 
N|               the doorway into the backyard. He is easy to identify through 
N|               the color of his garish necktie. In one hand the man carries 
N|               a small garden hoe and rake, and in the other a pair of 
N|               trimming shears. He goes to a small patch of flowers, perhaps 
N|               three feet square.
O|
N|               They are beautiful, multi-colored three foot high zinnias. 
N|               He kneels down, inspects them, touches them affectionately 
N|               and with some pride. His anger seems to have left him, 
N|               replaced by the kind of peace that flowers bring many people. 
N|               He stands up, carefully hoes the ground, them rakes it. Then 
N|               he snips a few leaves off the lower parts of the plant. 
N|               Finally, he waters them.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Jeff's attention is turned to something else of interest.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - DAY - SEMI-LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               Into the next door yard we see emerging from the apartment 
N|               below the ballet dancer, the elderly lady.
O|
N|               She wear a broad sun hat, dark glasses, and a sunsuit 
N|               consisting of pink shorts and halter. She carries a copy of 
N|               the Herald Tribune, and still wears her hearing aid. She 
N|               settles into a folding, canvas deck chair.
O|
N|               Her skin is dead white, and her body is thin to the point of 
N|               emaciation. No sooner has she settled into her chair, than 
N|               she is attracted by the sound of the salesman working in his 
N|               garden. She gets up, walks to the fence, and looks over. He 
N|               notices her, but doesn't speak.
O|
N|               She begins gesturing to him how to take care of his flowers. 
N|               He listens for a moment, then looks directly at her. The 
N|               strong movements of his mouth show us that he objects 
N|               vigorously to the annoyance of her comments. She moves away 
N|               from the fence, started and a little shocked.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - MEDIUM SHOT
O|
N|               Jeff is seated in the foreground, in a waist shot.
O|
N|               Behind him, the entrance door to his apartment opens.
O|
N,I|               STELLA McGAFFERY comes in. She is a husky, unhandsome, dark-
N,I|               haired woman who is dressed like a district nurse, with dark 
N,I|               coat, dark felt hat, with a white uniform showing underneath 
N,I|               the coat. She carries a small black bag.
O|
N|               Stella pauses on the landing to watch Jeff. He doesn't appear 
N|               to notice her entrance.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
P|                              (Loud)
U|                         The New York State sentence for a 
U|                         peeping Tom is six months in the 
U|                         workhouse!
O|
N|               He doesn't turn.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Hello Stella.
O|
N|               As she comes down the stairs of the landing, holding on the 
N|               wrought iron railing with one hand:
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         And there aren't any windows in the 
U|                         workhouse.
O|
N|               She puts her bag down on a table. It is worn, and looks as 
N|               if it belongs more to a fighter than a nurse. She takes off 
N|               her hat coat, and hangs them on a chair.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         Years ago, they used to put out your 
U|                         eyes with a hot poker. Is one of 
U|                         those bikini bombshells you always 
U|                         watch worth a hot poker?
O|
N|               He doesn't answer. She opens the bag, takes out some medical 
N|               supplies: a thermometer, a stop watch, a bottle of rubbing 
N|               oil, a can of powder, a towel. She talks as she works.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         We've grown to be a race of peeping 
U|                         Toms. What people should do is stand 
U|                         outside their own houses and look in 
U|                         once in a while.
P|                              (She looks up at him)
U|                         What do you think of that for homespun 
U|                         philosophy?
O|
N|               A look at his face shows he doesn't think much of it.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Readers' Digest, April, 1939.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         Well, I only quote from the best.
O|
N|               She takes the thermometer out of its case, shakes it down. 
N|               Looks at it. Satisfied, she walks to Jeff.
O|
N|               She swings the wheelchair around abruptly to face her.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - MEDIUM SHOT
O|
N|               Jeff starts to protest.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Now look, Stella --
O|
N|               She shoves the thermometer into his mouth.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         See it you can break a hundred.
O|
N,G|               As she leaves him holding the thermometer THE CAMERA PULLS 
N,G|               BACK as she crosses to a divan. She takes a sheet from 
N|               underneath, and covers the divan with it. Talking, all the 
N|               time.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         I shoulda been a Gypsy fortune teller, 
U|                         instead of an insurance company nurse. 
U|                         I got a nose for trouble -- can smell 
U|                         it ten miles away.
P|                              (Stops, looks at him)
U|                         You heard of the stock market crash 
U|                         in '29?
O|
N|               Jeff nods a bored "yes."
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         I predicted it.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (Around thermometer)
U|                         How?
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Stella stops for a moment, and looks at Jeff challengingly.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         Simple. I was nursing a director of 
U|                         General Motors. Kidney ailment they 
U|                         said. Nerves, I said. Then I asked 
U|                         myself -- what's General Motors got 
U|                         to be nervous about?
P|                              (Snaps her fingers)
U|                         Overproduction. Collapse, I answered. 
U|                         When General Motors has to go to the 
U|                         bathroom ten times a day -- the whole 
U|                         country's ready to let go.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - CLOSEUP
O|
N|               A patient, suffering look comes over his face. He takes out 
N|               the thermometer.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Stella -- in economics, a kidney 
U|                         ailment has no relationship to the 
U|                         stock market. Absolutely none.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         It crashed, didn't it?
O|
N|               Jeff has no answer. Defeated, he puts the thermometer back 
N|               into his mouth.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Stella goes on with her work.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         I can smell trouble right in this 
U|                         apartment. You broke your leg. You 
U|                         look out the window. You see things 
U|                         you shouldn't. Trouble. I can see 
U|                         you now, in front of the judge, 
U|                         flanked by lawyers in blue double-
U|                         breasted suits. You're pleading, 
U|                         "Judge, it was only innocent fun. I 
U|                         love my neighbors like a father." -- 
U|                         The Judge answers, "Congratulations. 
U|                         You just gave birth to three years 
U|                         in Dannemora."
O|
N,G|               THE CAMERA PANS HER over to him. She takes out the 
N|               thermometer, looks at it.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Right now I'd even welcome trouble.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
P|                              (Flatly)
U|                         You've got a hormone deficiency.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         How can you tell that from a 
U|                         thermometer!
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         Those sultry sun-worshipers you watch 
U|                         haven't raised your temperature one 
U|                         degree in four weeks.
O|
N|               She gets down the thermometer. Sterilizes it with a piece of 
N|               alcohol-soaked cotton in her other hand.
O|
N,G|               She gets behind the wheelchair the CAMERA PULLS back as she 
N|               pushes it over to the divan. She puts the thermometer away 
N|               in its case. Then she helps him off with his pajama top. She 
N|               helps him stand on one foot.
O|
N|               He hops one step, then she lowers him, face down, on the 
N|               divan. She gets a bottle of rubbing oil.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - DAY - CLOSE SHOT
O|
N,G|               The CAMERA is very low at one end of the divan. Jeff's head, 
N|               half-buried in the sheet, is large in the fore-ground.
O|
N|               Beyond him Stella looms large and powerful-looking.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         I think you're right. There is going 
U|                         to be some trouble around here.
O|
N|               Stella takes a handful of oil, slaps it on his back. He 
N|               winces.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         I knew it!
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Don't you ever heat that stuff up.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         Gives your circulation something to 
U|                         fight.
P|                              (Begins massaging his 
P|                              back)
U|                         What kind of trouble?
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Lisa Fremont.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         You must be kidding. A beautiful 
U|                         young woman, and you a reasonably 
U|                         healthy specimen of manhood.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         She expects me to marry her.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         That's normal.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         I don't want to.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
P|                              (Slaps cold oils on 
P|                              him)
U|                         That's abnormal.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (Wincing)
U|                         I'm not ready for marriage.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         Nonsense. A man is always ready for 
U|                         marriage -- with the right girl. And 
U|                         Lisa Fremont is the right girl for 
U|                         any man with half a brain, who can 
U|                         get one eye open.
O|
C|                                     JEFF
P|                              (Indifferent)
U|                         She's all right.
O|
N|               She hits him with some more cold oil. He winces again.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         Behind every ridiculous statement is 
U|                         always hidden the true cause.
P|                              (Peers at him)
U|                         What is it? You have a fight?
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         No.
O|
C|                                     STELLA
P|                              (After a pause)
U|                         Her father loading up the shotgun?
O|
C|                                     JEFF
U|                         Stella!
O|
C|                                     STELLA
U|                         It's happened before, you know! Some 
U|                         of the world's happiest marriage 
