S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - NIGHT - MEDIUM SHOT
O|
N|               The newlyweds's apartment has the shades still drawn.
O|
N|               Although there's a light burning inside.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - NIGHT - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
N|               There is a slight, but warm, smile on Jeff's face as he looks 
N|               at the drawn shade. His eyes move away from the newlyweds' 
N|               apartment, and slowly explore the neighborhood to his right. 
N|               He finds something of interest, and stops to stare at it. 
N|               His face sobers at what he sees.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - NIGHT - SEMI-LONG SHOT
O|
N,G|               The salesman's apartment. We see both the living room and 
N|               the bedroom. The salesman has prepared a dinner tray, and is 
N|               carrying it from the kitchen, through the lining room, into 
N|               the bedroom. He places it on the lap of his wife, sitting up 
N|               in bed. He puts a couple of pillows behind her back to make 
N|               her more comfortable.
O|
N|               She doesn't bother to thank him, but is busy examining the 
N|               content of the tray. Her attitude shows her dissatisfaction. 
N|               Nothing is right. It's not what she wanted, and it's badly 
N|               prepared. She begins criticizing him. He starts to answer 
N|               her back, but decides better of it, and instead, leaves the 
N|               room. He goes to the kitchen reaches up to a wall cabinet, 
N|               takes down a bottle and pours himself a drink. Then he returns 
N|               to the lining room, listens a moment. The wife is grudging 
N|               beginning to eat the dinner. The husband quietly lifts a 
N|               phone from the cradle, and dials a number.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - NIGHT - CLOSEUP
O|
N|               Jeff becomes completely absorbed with he sees. He leans 
N|               forward a little.
O|
S,G|               EXT. NEIGHBORHOOD - NIGHT - MEDIUM SHOT
O|
N,G|               We get a better view of the salesman waiting while his 
N|               connection is being made. Whoever he has called answers. And 
N|               instantly there is a marked change in his attitude. He 
N|               relaxes, smiles, is warm. He talks softly, perhaps guardedly, 
N|               with an occasional glance at the bedroom door. In the bedroom, 
N|               his wife has become aware of the call.
O|
N|               Quietly she moves the tray, gets out of bed, and goes to the 
N,G|               bedroom door to listen. The wall hides her from our view.
O|
N|               Then suddenly, she apparently opens the door, because the 
N,G|               living room, we see her arm suddenly appear, pointing at the 
N|               man and the telephone. He speaks quickly into the phone, and 
N|               hangs up. His face is flushed and angry as he goes toward 
N|               the bedroom. In the bedroom his wife appears walking back to 
N|               the bed, followed by the husband. She is laughing, and he is 
N|               answering her in angry tones. She climbs in bed laughing. 
N|               The more she laughs, the more angry he gets, and the harder 
N|               she laughs. Finally, he leaves the room, goes into the living 
N|               room, back into the kitchen and has another drink. He stands 
N|               there, controlling an outburst of emotion, and seems almost 
N|               to be crushing the shot glass in his clenched fist.
O|
S,G|               INT. JEFF'S APARTMENT - NIGHT - SEMI-CLOSEUP
O|
